    "title": "Artemis II will splashdown off San Diego coast with assistance from the Navy",
    "text": "The mission to the moon is nearing completion. Astronauts are already on their way back to Earth and are expected to splashdown at 5:07 p.m. Friday. SAN DIEGO — The historic mission to the moon is nearing completion - sending humans farther into space than ever before, giving us a look at the far side of the moon never seen before, and paving the way for a future mission to Mars. But there is one big part of the mission left. The splashdown. And that's where San Diego comes in, because of our nice weather and the region's Navy. CBS 8 spoke with the Captain at the helm of their return mission, which is set for Friday. As of Wednesday, the astronauts are already on their way back to Earth — right off the coast of San Diego, to be exact. Actual rocket science shows they'll splash down in the Pacific Ocean at 5:07 Friday evening. The Artemis II mission is leading the space race - blasting off from Kennedy Space Center in Florida, circling the far side of the moon and now soon splashing down off the coast of San Diego. \"We've been working on this with NASA for about 12 years, working up to this day,\" Captain Neil Krueger said, from Naval Base San Diego. \"They come in really fast, they have parachutes that deploy, so by the time they splash down in the water, they're only going about 20 miles per hour.\" Both Naval Base San Diego and Naval Air Station North Island are part of the recovery mission. 150 people from NASA and a couple of hundred local sailors on board the USS Murtha already made their way under the Coronado bridge and out to sea, to get into place. The amphibious ship has a transport dock, a helicopter pad and medical facilities. A special operations dive team and helicopter squadron will assist the four astronauts as they exit the Orion space capsule. \"We have mobile dive teams to open the capsule, get them onto a floating raft, helicopters will take them to the ship and get medical care,\" Krueger said. The next step is to recover the Orion capsule and tow it back onto the ship's well deck. It'll reach temperatures up to 3,000 degrees as it re-enters our atmosphere. \"There's falling debris coming from the capsule, stuff like that, that NASA has tools to monitor; we'll make sure public safety and wildlife safety is at the forefront of our mind,\" he said. From a ten-day mission around the moon to the Pacific Ocean - forging new frontiers for even deeper space missions. \"I've been in the Navy a long time; it's the most exciting thing I've been part of,\" Krueger said. Their return mission will be about 50-70 miles right off of San Diego's coast, and people could catch a glimpse of the ship when it returns to shore on Saturday morning.",
